<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Saxe-Meiningen</h1><p class="st-meta">1680 CE – 1918 CE · capital Meiningen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ernestine duchy in the Werra valley whose 'theatre duke' George II made its court troupe the most influential in Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernhard-i-duke-of-saxe-meiningen">Bernhard I</a>, third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-i-duke-of-saxe-gotha">Ernest the Pious</a>, received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a> in the 1680 partition of the Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> inheritance and made the small town his residence, building <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisabethenburg-palace">Elisabethenburg Palace</a>. The duchy later gained parts of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romhild">Römhild</a> and, in the 1826 redistribution, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildburghausen">Hildburghausen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The duchy was Lutheran and largely rural, with forestry and small industries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-ii-duke-of-saxe-meiningen">George II</a>, who reigned from 1866, personally directed the court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen-ensemble">Meininger</a> company toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from 1874 with historically accurate staging and disciplined ensemble acting, influencing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-stanislavski">Stanislavski</a>. The court orchestra under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-von-bulow">Hans von Bülow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritz-steinbach">Fritz Steinbach</a> was associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brahms">Johannes Brahms</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a> sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1866, forcing Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernhard-ii-duke-of-saxe-meiningen">Bernhard II</a> to abdicate in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-ii-duke-of-saxe-meiningen">George II</a>. It joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernhard-iii-duke-of-saxe-meiningen">Bernhard III</a> abdicated in November 1918, and in 1920 the state merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-state-of-thuringia">Free State of Thuringia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-saxe-meiningen.jpg" alt="Duchy of Saxe-Meiningen" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Saxe-Meiningen_(until_1818).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1680 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Meiningen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bernhard I, Bernhard II, George II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sachsen-Meiningen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1799&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-meiningen" aria-label="Duchy of Saxe-Meiningen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4918 422 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Saxe-Meiningen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5238 678L5244 667M5238 678L5229 694M5238 678L5199 691M5238 678L5300 622M5238 678L5250 665"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5244 667h0M5229 694h0M5199 691h0M5300 622h0M5250 665h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5238 678h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d